          "text": "The primary reason a CUDA graph replay is faster than eager kernel launching lies in the reduction of CPU-side overhead. In an eager execution model, every kernel launch involves a significant amount of work on the host CPU, including argument validation, memory management, and the submission of commands to the GPU driver. When launching thousands of small kernels, this CPU overhead can become the bottleneck, creating gaps in the GPU pipeline where the device sits idle waiting for the next command. CUDA graphs capture this sequence of operations into a single, pre-compiled execution plan. During replay, the CPU only needs to submit this single graph to the GPU, drastically reducing the host-to-device communication latency and allowing the GPU to execute the kernels with minimal interruption.\n\nFurthermore, CUDA graphs enable optimizations that are difficult or impossible to achieve with eager execution. Because the entire dependency graph is known ahead of time, the driver can optimize the scheduling of kernels, potentially overlapping memory transfers with computation or reordering independent kernels to maximize hardware utilization. This static nature also allows for more efficient memory allocation strategies, as the memory requirements for the entire graph can be determined and managed in advance, reducing the overhead associated with dynamic memory allocation during execution.\n\nHowever, using CUDA graphs comes with specific costs and limitations. The most significant cost is the",
